# Anti-CSRF Tokens in REST APIs & SPAs 🚀

Single Page Apps (React/Vue) don't use server-rendered HTML forms. How do they handle CSRF?

## Custom Request Header (`X-CSRF-Token`)

1. Server sets a readable cookie or returns the token via a bootstrap API.
2. Client JavaScript reads the token and attaches it to a custom HTTP request header:
   ```javascript_not
   fetch('/api/transfer', {
     method: 'POST',
     headers: {
       'Content-Type': 'application/json',
       'X-CSRF-Token': csrfToken,
     },
     body: JSON.stringify({ amount: 100 }),
   });
   ```

> ⚡ **Why This Works:** A simple cross-origin HTML form or image tag **cannot set custom HTTP headers** without triggering a CORS preflight which the server rejects!

# The Modern Standard: CSP `frame-ancestors` 📜

In modern web security, Content Security Policy's `frame-ancestors` directive supersedes `X-Frame-Options` with superior granularity.

```http_not
// 1. Equivalent to DENY (No one can frame this page)
Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none';

// 2. Equivalent to SAMEORIGIN
Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self';

// 3. Whitelist specific trusted partner domains:
Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self' https://trusted-partner.com https://checkout.store.com;
```

> ⚡ **Recommendation:** Send both `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN` and `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'` to protect legacy and modern browsers alike.
